1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,111
The thing keeping you broke

2
00:00:02,131 --> 00:00:04,085
probably isn't your salary.

3
00:00:04,105 --> 00:00:05,488
It's what happens to every

4
00:00:05,508 --> 00:00:06,658
raise you get.

5
00:00:06,678 --> 00:00:07,509
You earn more,

6
00:00:07,529 --> 00:00:08,409
you spend more,

7
00:00:08,429 --> 00:00:10,011
and somehow you never feel

8
00:00:10,031 --> 00:00:10,811
richer.

9
00:00:10,831 --> 00:00:11,805
It has a name:

10
00:00:11,825 --> 00:00:12,798
lifestyle creep.

11
00:00:12,818 --> 00:00:14,344
And today I'll show you

12
00:00:14,364 --> 00:00:15,393
how to beat it,

13
00:00:15,413 --> 00:00:17,559
without living like a monk.

14
00:00:17,579 --> 00:00:19,286
Lifestyle creep is when

15
00:00:19,306 --> 00:00:21,259
your spending quietly rises

16
00:00:21,279 --> 00:00:23,695
every time your income does.

17
00:00:23,715 --> 00:00:25,392
Nobody decides to do it.

18
00:00:25,412 --> 00:00:27,382
It happens one small upgrade

19
00:00:27,402 --> 00:00:28,543
at a time.

20
00:00:28,563 --> 00:00:29,794
A nicer apartment.

21
00:00:29,814 --> 00:00:30,687
A newer car.

22
00:00:30,707 --> 00:00:32,653
Takeout three nights a week

23
00:00:32,673 --> 00:00:34,125
instead of one.

24
00:00:34,145 --> 00:00:35,731
Each one feels reasonable

25
00:00:35,751 --> 00:00:36,881
on its own.

26
00:00:36,901 --> 00:00:38,001
But stack them up,

27
00:00:38,021 --> 00:00:39,478
and your whole raise is

28
00:00:39,498 --> 00:00:41,158
gone before it ever reaches

29
00:00:41,178 --> 00:00:42,322
your savings.

30
00:00:42,342 --> 00:00:43,572
The scary part is,

31
00:00:43,592 --> 00:00:44,651
you don't notice.

32
00:00:44,671 --> 00:00:46,469
Your life just slowly gets

33
00:00:46,489 --> 00:00:47,835
more expensive.

34
00:00:47,855 --> 00:00:49,847
Let's watch it happen.

35
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:50,733
Meet Sam.

36
00:00:50,753 --> 00:00:53,387
Sam earns $60,000 a year.

37
00:00:53,407 --> 00:00:55,989
Sam gets a $5,000 raise.

38
00:00:56,009 --> 00:00:56,967
Nice.

39
00:00:56,987 --> 00:00:57,967
After taxes,

40
00:00:57,987 --> 00:00:59,896
that's roughly $300 more

41
00:00:59,916 --> 00:01:00,611
a month,

42
00:01:00,631 --> 00:01:03,440
depending on where Sam lives.

43
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:04,332
Month one,

44
00:01:04,352 --> 00:01:06,787
Sam upgrades the phone plan.

45
00:01:06,807 --> 00:01:07,484
$40.

46
00:01:07,504 --> 00:01:08,282
Month two,

47
00:01:08,302 --> 00:01:10,344
a couple of new streaming

48
00:01:10,364 --> 00:01:12,473
services and a fancier gym.

49
00:01:12,493 --> 00:01:13,139
$70.

50
00:01:13,159 --> 00:01:14,384
Month four,

51
00:01:14,404 --> 00:01:16,683
a nicer car payment.

52
00:01:16,703 --> 00:01:17,562
$150.

53
00:01:17,582 --> 00:01:18,568
By month six,

54
00:01:18,588 --> 00:01:20,455
the rest goes to takeout.

55
00:01:20,475 --> 00:01:22,279
The whole $300 is spoken

56
00:01:22,299 --> 00:01:22,593
for,

57
00:01:22,613 --> 00:01:24,291
and Sam's savings didn't

58
00:01:24,311 --> 00:01:25,634
move at all.

59
00:01:25,654 --> 00:01:27,354
You might think this only

60
00:01:27,374 --> 00:01:29,130
happens to people on tight

61
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:29,629
budgets.

62
00:01:29,649 --> 00:01:31,583
The data says otherwise.

63
00:01:31,603 --> 00:01:32,319
Every year,

64
00:01:32,339 --> 00:01:33,961
the Federal Reserve asks

65
00:01:33,981 --> 00:01:35,774
Americans whether they have

66
00:01:35,794 --> 00:01:37,416
three months of expenses

67
00:01:37,436 --> 00:01:39,232
saved for emergencies.

68
00:01:39,252 --> 00:01:41,333
In its latest survey,

69
00:01:41,353 --> 00:01:43,939
only 21% of adults earning

70
00:01:43,959 --> 00:01:46,440
under $25,000 said yes.

71
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,923
At $100,000 or more,

72
00:01:48,943 --> 00:01:51,468
it jumps to 75%.

73
00:01:51,488 --> 00:01:52,759
That's a lot better.

74
00:01:52,779 --> 00:01:54,051
But flip it around:

75
00:01:54,071 --> 00:01:55,849
one in four people earning

76
00:01:55,869 --> 00:01:57,478
six figures still doesn't

77
00:01:57,498 --> 00:01:59,451
have three months saved.

78
00:01:59,471 --> 00:02:00,655
More income helps.

79
00:02:00,675 --> 00:02:02,317
It just doesn't guarantee

80
00:02:02,337 --> 00:02:02,890
anything,

81
00:02:02,910 --> 00:02:04,495
if spending rises right

82
00:02:04,515 --> 00:02:05,870
along with it.

83
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:07,373
Now zoom out to the whole

84
00:02:07,393 --> 00:02:08,196
country.

85
00:02:08,216 --> 00:02:09,833
In October 2026,

86
00:02:09,853 --> 00:02:12,245
Americans saved just 4.1%

87
00:02:12,265 --> 00:02:15,144
of their after-tax income.

88
00:02:15,164 --> 00:02:17,489
That means out of every

89
00:02:17,509 --> 00:02:17,894
$100,

90
00:02:17,914 --> 00:02:20,072
about $4 got saved.

91
00:02:20,092 --> 00:02:21,528
The other ninety-six got

92
00:02:21,548 --> 00:02:22,291
spent.

93
00:02:22,311 --> 00:02:23,653
So why does this happen

94
00:02:23,673 --> 00:02:24,945
to smart people?

95
00:02:24,965 --> 00:02:25,783
Reason one:

96
00:02:25,803 --> 00:02:27,974
you get used to things fast.

97
00:02:27,994 --> 00:02:30,164
Psychologists call it hedonic

98
00:02:30,184 --> 00:02:30,938
adaptation.

99
00:02:30,958 --> 00:02:32,935
The new car feels amazing

100
00:02:32,955 --> 00:02:33,902
for a month,

101
00:02:33,922 --> 00:02:36,106
then it's just your car.

102
00:02:36,126 --> 00:02:36,915
Reason two:

103
00:02:36,935 --> 00:02:38,905
your comparison group moves.

104
00:02:38,925 --> 00:02:40,212
Get a better job,

105
00:02:40,232 --> 00:02:42,203
and suddenly your coworkers

106
00:02:42,223 --> 00:02:44,131
drive nicer cars and take

107
00:02:44,151 --> 00:02:45,464
bigger trips.

108
00:02:45,484 --> 00:02:46,585
Reason three:

109
00:02:46,605 --> 00:02:49,275
upgrades come in small monthly

110
00:02:49,295 --> 00:02:49,798
bites.

111
00:02:49,818 --> 00:02:50,546
$15 here,

112
00:02:50,566 --> 00:02:51,368
$40 there.

113
00:02:51,388 --> 00:02:53,535
None of them feels like

114
00:02:53,555 --> 00:02:54,906
a decision.

115
00:02:54,926 --> 00:02:56,006
And reason four:

116
00:02:56,026 --> 00:02:56,990
you earned it.

117
00:02:57,010 --> 00:02:57,858
And you did!

118
00:02:57,878 --> 00:02:59,710
But treating yourself every

119
00:02:59,730 --> 00:03:01,330
single month turns into

120
00:03:01,350 --> 00:03:02,888
a permanent bill.

121
00:03:02,908 --> 00:03:04,443
Here's what lifestyle creep

122
00:03:04,463 --> 00:03:05,596
really costs.

123
00:03:05,616 --> 00:03:07,746
Say Sam had invested that

124
00:03:07,766 --> 00:03:09,810
$300 a month instead.

125
00:03:09,830 --> 00:03:12,091
At a 7% yearly return,

126
00:03:12,111 --> 00:03:14,460
which is an assumption,

127
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:16,127
not a guarantee,

128
00:03:16,147 --> 00:03:18,935
that grows to about $366,000

129
00:03:18,955 --> 00:03:20,651
in 30 years.

130
00:03:20,671 --> 00:03:23,026
Sam only put in $108,000.

131
00:03:23,046 --> 00:03:25,378
The rest is growth.

132
00:03:25,398 --> 00:03:27,011
And that's from one raise.

133
00:03:27,031 --> 00:03:28,807
Most people get many raises

134
00:03:28,827 --> 00:03:30,133
over a career.

135
00:03:30,153 --> 00:03:32,245
Every raise you spend completely

136
00:03:32,265 --> 00:03:34,024
is a piece of your future

137
00:03:34,044 --> 00:03:36,092
you're quietly giving away.

138
00:03:36,112 --> 00:03:37,645
Here are three warning signs

139
00:03:37,665 --> 00:03:39,188
it's happening to you.

140
00:03:39,208 --> 00:03:39,439
One:

141
00:03:39,459 --> 00:03:41,096
you earn more than you did

142
00:03:41,116 --> 00:03:42,050
three years ago,

143
00:03:42,070 --> 00:03:43,507
but your savings balance

144
00:03:43,527 --> 00:03:45,162
looks about the same.

145
00:03:45,182 --> 00:03:45,413
Two:

146
00:03:45,433 --> 00:03:46,868
you can't say where last

147
00:03:46,888 --> 00:03:48,373
month's money went without

148
00:03:48,393 --> 00:03:50,228
opening your banking app.

149
00:03:50,248 --> 00:03:50,634
Three:

150
00:03:50,654 --> 00:03:51,792
your fixed bills,

151
00:03:51,812 --> 00:03:52,487
like rent,

152
00:03:52,507 --> 00:03:54,283
your car and subscriptions,

153
00:03:54,303 --> 00:03:55,963
went up every time your

154
00:03:55,983 --> 00:03:56,942
pay did.

155
00:03:56,962 --> 00:03:58,899
If any of those sound familiar,

156
00:03:58,919 --> 00:03:59,693
you're normal.

157
00:03:59,713 --> 00:04:01,098
And it's fixable.

158
00:04:01,118 --> 00:04:02,660
So how do you beat it?

159
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:04,111
Not by never spending.

160
00:04:04,131 --> 00:04:05,961
Here's a simple system.

161
00:04:05,981 --> 00:04:06,600
Step one:

162
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:08,343
the fifty-fifty raise rule.

163
00:04:08,363 --> 00:04:10,260
Every time you get a raise,

164
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:12,002
half goes to your future,

165
00:04:12,022 --> 00:04:14,319
and half goes to your life.

166
00:04:14,339 --> 00:04:16,759
Sam would still get $150

167
00:04:16,779 --> 00:04:19,031
a month more to enjoy,

168
00:04:19,051 --> 00:04:21,535
and still invest $150.

169
00:04:21,555 --> 00:04:23,371
Even that half,

170
00:04:23,391 --> 00:04:25,717
at 7% for 30 years,

171
00:04:25,737 --> 00:04:29,382
could grow to about $183,000.

172
00:04:29,402 --> 00:04:30,129
Step two:

173
00:04:30,149 --> 00:04:32,303
automate it before you see

174
00:04:32,323 --> 00:04:32,575
it.

175
00:04:32,595 --> 00:04:34,682
Raise your 401(k) percentage,

176
00:04:34,702 --> 00:04:37,264
or set up an automatic transfer,

177
00:04:37,284 --> 00:04:39,234
the same week the raise

178
00:04:39,254 --> 00:04:40,178
starts.

179
00:04:40,198 --> 00:04:42,113
Money you never see in checking

180
00:04:42,133 --> 00:04:43,939
is money you won't miss.

181
00:04:43,959 --> 00:04:45,790
This works because you never

182
00:04:45,810 --> 00:04:46,824
feel a pay cut.

183
00:04:46,844 --> 00:04:48,620
Your life still gets better,

184
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:50,436
just not all at once.

185
00:04:50,456 --> 00:04:51,160
Step three:

186
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:52,386
upgrade on purpose.

187
00:04:52,406 --> 00:04:54,225
Pick the one or two things

188
00:04:54,245 --> 00:04:56,064
that truly make you happier,

189
00:04:56,084 --> 00:04:57,847
and spend on those without

190
00:04:57,867 --> 00:04:58,637
guilt.

191
00:04:58,657 --> 00:05:00,179
Cut the upgrades that just

192
00:05:00,199 --> 00:05:01,109
crept in.

193
00:05:01,129 --> 00:05:01,790
Step four:

194
00:05:01,810 --> 00:05:02,925
a yearly check-up.

195
00:05:02,945 --> 00:05:03,776
Once a year,

196
00:05:03,796 --> 00:05:05,534
look at your subscriptions

197
00:05:05,554 --> 00:05:06,612
and fixed bills,

198
00:05:06,632 --> 00:05:07,180
and ask:

199
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:08,938
would I sign up for this

200
00:05:08,958 --> 00:05:10,133
again today?

201
00:05:10,153 --> 00:05:11,408
If the answer is no,

202
00:05:11,428 --> 00:05:12,420
cancel it.

203
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:13,803
Let's recap.

204
00:05:13,823 --> 00:05:15,633
Lifestyle creep is spending

205
00:05:15,653 --> 00:05:17,921
that rises with every raise.

206
00:05:17,941 --> 00:05:19,788
Even one in four six-figure

207
00:05:19,808 --> 00:05:21,538
earners doesn't have three

208
00:05:21,558 --> 00:05:22,814
months saved.

209
00:05:22,834 --> 00:05:23,544
One raise,

210
00:05:23,564 --> 00:05:24,153
invested,

211
00:05:24,173 --> 00:05:26,039
could grow to hundreds of

212
00:05:26,059 --> 00:05:27,109
thousands.

213
00:05:27,129 --> 00:05:29,088
Split every raise fifty-fifty,

214
00:05:29,108 --> 00:05:29,928
automate it,

215
00:05:29,948 --> 00:05:31,948
and upgrade on purpose.

216
00:05:31,968 --> 00:05:32,739
Next up:

217
00:05:32,759 --> 00:05:34,402
Roth IRA vs 401(k),

218
00:05:34,422 --> 00:05:36,856
and where your first $1,000

219
00:05:36,876 --> 00:05:37,805
should go.

220
00:05:37,825 --> 00:05:39,927
See you there.
